Firstly, I would like to apologise to all my readers, that I was not posting anything, and not letting anyone know about this. There were so many reasons, and I do not want to go into detail about it now. For short, I was really busy, and I was fed up with the state of my old site. I think that is the main reason I was not posting anything at all — I even tried to post on Facebook instead. There are so many things I could've done better, but heck, I've learnt from my mistakes :-)

So to the main topic of this post: after almost a year of analysing, designing and development, I managed to finish a production build of my site! Here, I proudly present you my brand-new site!

{% R2Image
   src="https://ik.imagekit.io/8qy7obkhf/haikonguyen-eu/blog/2020/welcome-to-my-new-website/image-01.jpg"
   alt="Sketch of the new website design"
   caption="One of the early design sketches for the rebuild" /%}

I created this blog in 2015 during my last days as a student at the University of Economics. During this time, I was really interested in photography and web development. My first post ever was about photography.

### The old DEV stack

- Wordpress
- jQuery
- HTML
- CSS
- PHP
- MySQL

I've been managing this blog for quite some time now, and at first it was a joy — I spent countless hours learning and practicing the Wordpress ecosystem and became quite familiar with it. I was lucky enough to build many sites based on this system professionally and personally. Besides the front-end technologies, I learnt a bit of the WordPress templating language based on PHP along the way :-)

Well, after a few years with the WordPress site, I found that my blog was getting quite slow for what it does, which is basically only serving my articles. Plus, it was not so optimised for mobile devices, and the site was not overall secure because it was based on the old HTTP protocol. As a design fan, I wanted to create something of my own — and I think I did it!

### Reasons to recreate the site

- Speed
- Security
- Old and not responsive design
- Learning new technologies along the way :-)

### The new DEV stack

- GatsbyJS
- Styled Components
- React
- Material-UI
- GraphQL
- NetlifyCMS and Netlify

I've been interested in Gatsby for some time, and working as a front-end developer I got a chance to work on these new technologies in practice while I was at Starky's Club. In fact, my first project there was to rebuild the company's site into Gatsby.

So, one day when I was researching the web, I ended up at [taniarascia.com](https://www.taniarascia.com/). Tania Rascia is an amazing tech writer, self-taught professional software engineer and tutor. When I first saw her page I was fascinated by the speed — everything was buttery smooth and snappy. I spent quite some time browsing her blog and subscribed immediately. Reading her writing, I found she has a background quite close to mine, and her site was exceptional. That day I told myself I need to make something like that with my site. Next steps were pretty easy: follow her article about migrating a WordPress site to Gatsby, research each technology, and that was it.

### Design

I am not a designer by profession, but for a long time I really liked Google's Material Design. Personally I think it was made for the web, and Google did a great job creating a masterpiece blueprint. So I knew in my mind it should be based on Material Design. I started to draw sketches and wireframes, and designed my site based on that blueprint. That's basically my workflow for the designing process.

{% R2Image
   src="https://ik.imagekit.io/8qy7obkhf/haikonguyen-eu/blog/2020/welcome-to-my-new-website/image-02.jpg"
   alt="Website rebuild in progress"
   caption="Building the new stack — Gatsby, React, and Material-UI" /%}

### Development

This stage was, as you can guess, the most time-consuming one. The good thing is that there are so many cool tools that helped speed up the process. Material-UI is a React library for Google's Material Design components — great for rapid UI development if you know Twitter's Bootstrap; this one is very similar.

Then there is GatsbyJS, which is the main reason I started all of this. One of Gatsby's slogans is "Create blazing fast websites and apps", and now that I've completed the rebuilding process I think this framework really delivers.

I won't go into detail about each technology in the new DEV stack, but I can tell you that it was fun and rewarding, with very interesting knowledge — the main reason that kept me hooked to code and finish this site. To be honest, sometimes it was really frustrating and making me go crazy, but after all those nights with less sleep, it was very worth it. If you are planning to do something similar, I would totally recommend it! Just be patient, and keep coding!

### Closing thoughts

Overall, I am very happy that I've managed to complete this quite time-consuming task. It is one of my accomplishments that I am very proud of and want to share with you all. The site is not perfect — there are a few bugs here and there — and by visiting it, you can help me make it better!

In the future, I am planning to create more interesting articles about coding, photography, video-making, and so on.

So, follow me and keep reading! I hope each of you will find something interesting!
